Q: About using react-router-v5 on Electron A: <p>Routes are matched from top to bottom, and the first matching route renders.</p>
<p>Since your <code>&quot;/&quot;</code> path matches every route (because <code>/contact</code> and <code>/about</code> both start with <code>/</code>), it always renders the <code>Homepage</code> component.</p>
<p>Instead you can reorder the <code>Switch</code> configuration as follows:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;Switch&gt;
  &lt;Route path=&quot;/contact&quot;&gt;
    &lt;Contact /&gt;
  &lt;/Route&gt;
  &lt;Route path=&quot;/about&quot;&gt;
    &lt;About /&gt;
  &lt;/Route&gt;
  &lt;Route path=&quot;/&quot;&gt;
    &lt;Homepage /&gt;
  &lt;/Route&gt;
&lt;/Switch&gt;
</code></pre>

Q: How to dynamically change window title in iced.rs? A: <h2>Updated for iced <code>0.14</code></h2>
<p>In <code>iced = &quot;0.14&quot;</code> the signature of the <a href="https://docs.rs/iced/latest/iced/fn.run.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>iced::run</code></a> and <a href="https://docs.rs/iced/latest/iced/fn.application.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>iced::application</code></a> functions were changed such that the title was removed. Therefore the <a href="https://docs.rs/iced/latest/iced/application/struct.Application.html#method.title" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>title</code></a> function of the <a href="https://docs.rs/iced/latest/iced/application/struct.Application.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>Application</code></a> must now be used instead as demonstrated below:</p>
<pre class="lang-rust prettyprint-override"><code>use iced::{
    Center, Element, Length, Task,
    widget::{Space, button, column, row, text},
};
use std::time::{SystemTime, UNIX_EPOCH};

pub fn main() -&gt; iced::Result {
    iced::application(
        DynamicTitle::default,
        DynamicTitle::update,
        DynamicTitle::view,
    )
    .title(DynamicTitle::title)
    .run()
}

struct DynamicTitle {
    title: String,
}

impl Default for DynamicTitle {
    fn default() -&gt; Self {
        Self {
            title: &quot;Default Title&quot;.to_string(),
        }
    }
}

#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy)]
enum Message {
    ChangeTitle,
}

impl DynamicTitle {
    fn title(&amp;self) -&gt; String {
        self.title.clone()
    }

    fn update(&amp;mut self, message: Message) -&gt; Task&lt;Message&gt; {
        match message {
            Message::ChangeTitle =&gt; {
                let now = SystemTime::now();
                let unix = now.duration_since(UNIX_EPOCH).unwrap();
                self.title = format!(&quot;Unix timestamp: {}&quot;, unix.as_secs());
            }
        }
        Task::none()
    }

    fn view(&amp;self) -&gt; Element&lt;'_, Message&gt; {
        row![
            Space::new().width(Length::Fill),
            column![
                Space::new().height(Length::Fill),
                button(&quot;Change Title&quot;).on_press(Message::ChangeTitle),
                text(self.title.clone()).size(30),
                Space::new().height(Length::Fill),
            ]
            .padding(20)
            .align_x(Center),
            Space::new().width(Length::Fill),
        ]
        .into()
    }
}
</code></pre>
<h2>Original Answer</h2>
<p>You can pass a function that returns a <code>String</code> to the <a href="https://docs.rs/iced/latest/iced/fn.application.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>iced::application</code></a> function like so:</p>
<pre class="lang-rust prettyprint-override"><code>pub fn main() -&gt; iced::Result {
    iced::application(
        DynamicTitle::title,
        DynamicTitle::update,
        DynamicTitle::view,
    ).run()
}
</code></pre>
<p>Then define your <code>title</code> function for <code>DynamicTitle</code> like so:</p>
<pre class="lang-rust prettyprint-override"><code>impl DynamicTitle {
    fn title(&amp;self) -&gt; String {
        &quot;My Title&quot;.to_string()
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>If you want to change the value using a <code>Message</code> you will need to store your <code>title</code> on your <em>application state</em>.</p>
<p>Here is a basic sample where the window title is set to the current unix timestamp when the &quot;Change Title&quot; button is pressed:</p>
<pre class="lang-rust prettyprint-override"><code>use iced::{
    Center, Element, Length,
    widget::{Space, button, column, row, text},
};
use std::time::{SystemTime, UNIX_EPOCH};

pub fn main() -&gt; iced::Result {
    iced::run(
        DynamicTitle::title,
        DynamicTitle::update,
        DynamicTitle::view,
    )
}

struct DynamicTitle {
    title: String,
}

impl Default for DynamicTitle {
    fn default() -&gt; Self {
        Self {
            title: &quot;Default Title&quot;.to_string(),
        }
    }
}

#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy)]
enum Message {
    ChangeTitle,
}

impl DynamicTitle {
    fn title(&amp;self) -&gt; String {
        self.title.clone()
    }

    fn update(&amp;mut self, message: Message) {
        match message {
            Message::ChangeTitle =&gt; {
                let now = SystemTime::now();
                let unix = now.duration_since(UNIX_EPOCH).unwrap();
                self.title = format!(&quot;Unix timestamp: {}&quot;, unix.as_secs()).to_string();
            }
        }
    }

    fn view(&amp;self) -&gt; Element&lt;'_, Message&gt; {
        row![
            Space::with_width(Length::Fill),
            column![
                Space::with_height(Length::Fill),
                button(&quot;Change Title&quot;).on_press(Message::ChangeTitle),
                text(self.title.clone()).size(30),
                Space::with_height(Length::Fill),
            ]
            .padding(20)
            .align_x(Center),
            Space::with_width(Length::Fill),
        ]
        .into()
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: Center the angular MatDialog A: <p>Ive tested the following with Angular 17:</p>
<p><strong>1) Add Angular material theme (angular.json)</strong></p>
<pre><code>&quot;styles&quot;: [
  &quot;@angular/material/prebuilt-themes/deeppurple-amber.css&quot;,
  &quot;src/styles.scss&quot;
]
</code></pre>
<p>If you dont have a theme the CDK dialog css doesnt correctlyy apply and you can getthe  the  behavior in screenshot.</p>
<p><strong>2) Make sure animations is enabled (app.config.ts)</strong></p>
<pre><code>import { ApplicationConfig, importProvidersFrom } from '@angular/core';
import { provideAnimationsAsync } from '@angular/platform-browser/animations/async';
import { MatDialogModule } from '@angular/material/dialog';

export const appConfig: ApplicationConfig = {
  providers: [provideAnimationsAsync(), importProvidersFrom(MatDialogModule)]
};
</code></pre>
<p><strong>3) Fix the `MAT_DIALOG_DATA` import</strong></p>
<p>Change:</p>
<p>import { MAT_DIALOG_DATA } from &quot;@angular/material&quot;;</p>
<p>To:</p>
<p>import { MAT_DIALOG_DATA } from &quot;@angular/material/dialog&quot;;</p>
